The code statements that lead to the bug are:
```c
QTAILQ_FOREACH(entry, &spapr->pending_events, next) {
    QTAILQ_REMOVE(&spapr->pending_events, entry, next);
}
```
Reason:
Using `QTAILQ_FOREACH()` to iterate over the queue while simultaneously removing elements can cause the iterator to access an element that has already been removed. This results in a user-after-free error, as the loop may attempt to dereference a pointer to a freed memory location, leading to undefined behavior.

The code statements that fix the bug are:
```c
QTAILQ_FOREACH_SAFE(entry, &spapr->pending_events, next, next_entry) {
    QTAILQ_REMOVE(&spapr->pending_events, entry, next);
}
```
Reason:
The use of `QTAILQ_FOREACH_SAFE()` allows for safe iteration and removal of elements from the queue. It ensures that the next element is stored in `next_entry` before the current `entry` is removed, preventing access to a freed memory location and thus eliminating the user-after-free error.

